    technicalDeepDive: 'Shrimp muscle is thin and dense with fast-contracting fibers, so once internal protein denatures past 140°F the flesh tightens and shortens along its length, squeezing bound water out as it goes; pulling at 145°F catches that contraction early, before it has pressed too much moisture free. Because the shrimp are patted dry before oiling, there is no surface water left to absorb incoming energy as latent heat, so the rapid convective air at 400°F drives surface temperature into browning range within the first couple of minutes despite the short overall window. Their small mass and high surface-to-volume ratio mean conduction reaches the center almost as fast as the surface heats, leaving little margin between raw and tough.',
