    technicalDeepDive: 'This roast carries almost no marbling or connective tissue to render and self-baste the meat as it cooks, so the margin between juicy and dry is unusually narrow, and pulling at 140°F rather than cooking straight through to 145°F is what keeps it inside that margin. Because the tenderloin is a slim, even cylinder, heat conducts inward from all sides at a similar rate, which is why the resting period afterward can carry the center up those last several degrees through residual conduction alone rather than needing more time in the chamber. The high 400°F air temperature compensates for the short cook window by pushing surface browning fast enough to build a crust before the lean interior has a chance to overcook from the inside out.',

