    bonusFaqs: [
      {
        q: 'Why do frozen fries from an air fryer taste crispier than fries from an oven?',
        a: 'An oven surrounds the fries with still, radiant heat that browns the outside but does not remove moisture from the interior as efficiently. An air fryer\'s circulating convective air at 400°F pulls moisture off the surface continuously, so the surface dehydrates completely while the interior stays dry. Oven fries need much higher temperatures and longer time to achieve the same dehydration, and by then the exterior has usually darkened too much.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I make fresh-cut fries in the air fryer instead of buying frozen ones?',
        a: 'Yes, but the time is very different. Fresh-cut fries that are not par-fried need 20–25 minutes at 380°F because there is no pre-set crust and much more moisture to drive off. Start checking at 18 minutes. They will never crisp as fast as frozen par-fried fries because they are starting from completely raw starch.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Should I add salt before or after cooking?',
        a: 'Add salt after cooking, while the fries are still hot and slightly damp. Salt applied before cooking will sink into the moisture on the surface and clump, or it will fall off during shaking. Salt applied immediately after pull adheres to the residual heat and oil coating and stays on the fry.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I reheat leftover fries in the air fryer?',
        a: 'Yes, and it works remarkably well. Preheat to 350°F, load the cold fries loosely in the basket (do not shake them, just let them sit), and cook for 3–4 minutes. The convective air rehydrates the fries just enough to soften them to their original texture while re-crisping the exterior. Do not overfill or they\'ll steam instead of reheat.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Is it okay to cook fries and chicken wings in the same batch?',
        a: 'Not recommended. Wings cook best at 380–390°F and need 18–20 minutes, while fries need 400°F and 14–16 minutes. If you try to split the difference, one will undercook and the other may burn. Cook them separately or use two air fryers.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why do some fries come out burnt even though I followed the time exactly?',
        a: 'Air fryer basket wear, uneven heating elements, and variations in the depth of par-frying all mean that 14–16 minutes is a range, not exact. Checking fries by visual appearance (golden color, not dark brown) and interior texture (dry, not wet) is more reliable than time. Also, opening the basket to shake lets cool air in, so the first batch you make in a day might finish 1–2 minutes faster than subsequent batches.',
      },
    ],
    technicalDeepDive: 'Commercial frozen fries are already par-fried, so the starch on their surface gelatinized once in the factory and set into a thin crust before freezing; the air fryer\'s real job is dehydrating and re-crisping that existing crust, not cooking raw potato from scratch. Ice crystals on the surface absorb the first few minutes of convective energy as they melt, which is why the fries still look soft even at a steady 400°F, and only start browning once that surface water is gone. Because so little internal moisture remains after par-frying, once surface temperature clears the Maillard threshold it browns fast, so much of the later cook time is about driving off residual surface humidity so the crust locks in as the fries cool.',

