    technicalDeepDive: 'A raw potato\'s dense flesh conducts heat slowly, which is why a whole 8–10 oz russet needs the better part of 45 minutes at 400°F for the center to catch up to the surface rather than the few minutes a thinner vegetable would need. Getting the core past roughly 205°F matters because starch granules inside the cells only fully gelatinize and burst open in that upper range, converting the dense raw structure into the light, separated texture that reads as fluffy rather than gluey or waxy. The oil-rubbed skin, meanwhile, loses water fast enough under sustained convection to dehydrate into a genuinely crisp shell instead of a merely softened jacket, since the moving air keeps stripping away moisture as it migrates outward from the interior.',

