    technicalDeepDive: 'Dumpling wrappers are a thin, pre-gelatinized starch sheet, and without fat against that surface they would simply dry and toughen in the fryer rather than brown and firm up, which is part of why the prep coats both faces in oil, giving the quickly circulating air something to conduct heat through evenly and mimicking the effect of shallow frying. The 165°F target reflects that these are mixed ground fillings that can include poultry, so the higher USDA poultry minimum governs the whole dumpling rather than the lower threshold that would apply to a pork-only filling. Because the filling is fully sealed inside the wrapper, steam builds inside that pocket as the water in the meat heats, and it\'s this trapped steam that finishes cooking the center of the filling even as the exterior handles browning on its own track.',

