    donenessCue: 'A paring knife slides into the thickest carrot with no resistance; edges are caramelized where they touched the pan and the honey-butter has reduced to a sticky glaze pooling around them, not a thin watery layer.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Toss the honey and butter with the carrots at the halfway flip, not at the start. Honey put on a raw carrot at minute zero will scorch and turn bitter well before the carrot is tender; adding it partway means the sugars caramelize just as the carrots finish, not 15 minutes ahead of them.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — carrots are judged by knife-tender texture and glaze reduction, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 400°F/22–28 minute window reflects standard convection roasting for whole medium carrots or lengthwise halves; baby carrots or coins finish 6–8 minutes sooner.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Carrot rigidity comes from pectin cementing cell walls together and cellulose reinforcing them from within, and neither breaks down quickly — getting a knife to slide in with no resistance takes a sustained 22 to 28 minutes at 400°F, roughly the same order of time raw potatoes need, since both are dense roots built the same structural way. Carrots also carry their own natural sugars independent of the added honey, so even an unglazed carrot would caramelize somewhat at the cut surface; the honey and butter mostly layer a second, faster-caramelizing sugar source on top of what the vegetable already does. Whole carrots present less cut surface than halved ones, so they lean more heavily on the glaze for color.',
