    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Do not salt the zucchini in advance and do not crowd the pan. Zucchini is roughly 95% water — salt it early and it weeps onto the pan; crowd it and the escaping steam has nowhere to go, so you get boiled zucchini instead of roasted. Season right before it goes in, and give each piece air.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — zucchini is judged by caramelization and structural integrity, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 425°F window is chosen to drive moisture off fast enough that browning wins over steaming; a lower temp turns zucchini to grey mush.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Cut zucchini flesh can\'t begin real Maillard browning until its surface moisture is gone, because evaporating water absorbs enough energy to pin the surface near the boiling point while it escapes; only once that surface runs dry does it have thermal headroom to brown instead of steam. The skin side, protected by a waxy cuticle, loses moisture far more slowly than the cut faces, so it stays pale while the two cut faces of each half-moon do nearly all the browning work. On a thin sheet pan, the face touching the metal gets fast conductive heat while the top face depends on slower convective air, which is why flipping partway changes which face ends up caramelized.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'sheet-pan-roasted-zucchini',
    keywords: ['how long to roast zucchini', 'sheet pan zucchini time', 'roasted zucchini temperature', 'why is my roasted zucchini soggy', 'zucchini oven temperature'],
