    proTip: 'Cut through the core, not around it. The core is the only thing holding the leaves together as concentric rings — cut it out and the steak falls apart into wilted strips halfway through cooking. A steak with the core intact stays a steak, and the core softens enough to eat by the time the leaves are crisp.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — cabbage steaks are judged by cut-face caramelization and fork-tender core, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 28–32 minute window at 425°F reflects the time needed for the core, which is the last part to soften, to become tender through a 1-inch steak.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A cabbage steak is really several concentric leaf layers pressed together, and heat has to conduct from one water-filled layer into the next to reach the core; loosely packed outer leaves, separated by thin pockets of insulating air, heat faster than the tightly wound inner layers where cell walls sit in direct contact. That\'s why the outer leaves crisp into something like chips well before the dense, high-moisture core near the stem has softened — that core is essentially a knob of pectin-bound tissue, and pectin breakdown under sustained oven heat is slow and steady no matter how hot the surface gets. The result is one vegetable running two timelines: browned and crisp at the edges, still gelatinizing toward tender at the center.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'sheet-pan-roasted-cabbage-steaks',
    keywords: ['how long to roast cabbage steaks', 'sheet pan cabbage time', 'roasted cabbage temperature', 'cabbage steaks oven', 'how to cut cabbage steaks'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to roast cabbage steaks: 425°F (218°C) for 28–32 minutes. Why to cut through the core, not around it.',
