    technicalDeepDive: 'A frozen taquito is a thin corn tortilla wrapped around a core of meat that starts well below freezing, so the two components heat on different timelines inside the same 380°F airstream. The tortilla\'s low mass lets its surface starches dextrinize and brown within the first few minutes, but the meat core must first absorb enough conducted energy to thaw its ice before climbing toward the 165°F point USDA sets for poultry filling — a slower process governed by conduction through dense protein rather than surface convection. That mismatch is why total time is set by the core, even though the wrapper looks finished well before the filling catches up.',

