      'Pale smoke should rise continuously from the pan throughout the sear. If the sizzle goes quiet or the smoke thins, the pan temperature has dipped below the Maillard window and is recovering slowly. For this lean cut, a wavering sizzle is a sign the sear is stalling before the crust sets.',
      'After resting, a light shake of the plated steak should show the meat wobbling slightly within its crust. This movement signals the interior is still actively hot and carryover cooking has completed, bringing the center to the rested temperature. A stiff, immobile steak means the interior has cooled and lost residual heat.',
      'The steak\'s interior feel under gentle fingertip pressure changes as it cooks. A raw tenderloin feels soft and putty-like; at 130°F target, the center should yield slightly when pressed and spring back partially, but never feel spongy or depressed. A lean cut won\'t bounce back like a fattier steak, so the expectation is subtle give, not dramatic rebound.',
