    technicalDeepDive: 'Center-cut tenderloin carries almost none of the intramuscular fat that a ribeye has, so there\'s no internal basting happening as it cooks, and the exterior needs oil and butter to supply what marbling would otherwise provide. At two inches thick, the sear has to run longer to walk 130°F all the way to the core, since this cut has no fat cap to shield its exterior from drying out the way a fattier steak\'s does. Because the pan\'s heat reserve is so large relative to the meat, contact with the cold filet costs it very little surface temperature, so the crust keeps building through both two-minute sides even though this lean cut offers no fat of its own to help it along.',

