    technicalDeepDive: 'Sausage casing acts as a semi-permeable membrane, so as pork fat renders during the 400°F cook it stays largely contained and continuously bastes the meat from within rather than pooling onto the pan. That containment is also why an unpricked casing browns and blisters through direct Maillard exposure at the surface while the fat and juice inside stay emulsified rather than separating out. The full 25-30 minutes gives that fat time to fully render toward the core and the myosin network time to set past 160°F, longer than a similarly sized cut would need because the casing slows how fast internal steam can vent.',
