    technicalDeepDive: 'Corn kernels are individually small and wrapped in a thin pericarp, so unlike a dense root vegetable there is very little mass for heat to penetrate. Four to six minutes in boiling water is enough for conducted heat to soften the starch inside each kernel without needing to travel far through the ear. The kernels are also mostly intact cellulose and starch structures rather than raw tissue that needs prolonged softening, so the limiting factor is warming the interior evenly rather than breaking down tough connective structures. Pull the ear early and the center of each kernel stays starchy and dense instead of turning tender and plump.',
