    technicalDeepDive: 'Carrot roots stay rigid because pectin cements adjacent cell walls together at the middle lamella, and a sustained 212°F bath slowly de-esterifies that pectin, dissolving its calcium cross-links so cells separate rather than rupture. A 1/4-inch round is thin enough that conductive heat saturates the interior within a minute or so, meaning most of the 5-8 minute window is pectin chemistry playing out, not heat working its way to the center. That\'s also why doubling the thickness of a coin doesn\'t double the cook time proportionally — the limiting step is how long the pectin sits at 212°F, not how far heat has to travel to reach it.',
