    technicalDeepDive: 'Rice starch sits in semicrystalline granules that hold their shape until enough thermal energy disrupts the bonding holding them together, at which point they absorb water and swell irreversibly — the gelatinization that turns a hard grain tender. Because water caps out at 212°F regardless of burner setting, dropping to a 200°F simmer after the initial boil doesn\'t slow that chemistry meaningfully; it mainly softens the bubbling, which at a full rolling boil would knock grains against each other hard enough to abrade starch off their exterior and cloud the liquid. The 1:2 ratio matters because the grain takes up water through capillary action into its porous starch matrix, and too little liquid leaves the center under-gelatinized before the outside runs dry.',
