    technicalDeepDive: 'A live mussel stays clamped shut because its adductor muscle holds a contraction, and what opens the shell isn\'t force but heat denaturing the proteins in that muscle until it can no longer hold on. Cooking covered in a shallow pool of wine or broth means most of the heat reaching each shell arrives as condensing steam rather than direct liquid contact, and steam releases far more energy per gram on contact than boiling water at the same temperature, because of the latent heat given up when vapor turns back to liquid. That\'s what lets a few minutes heat every mussel evenly despite their stacked, irregular shapes in the pot.',
