    technicalDeepDive: 'Raw asparagus looks duller than cooked because air trapped between its cells scatters light before it reaches the chlorophyll; heat ruptures the membranes holding those air pockets, purging the trapped gas and letting light travel straight to the pigment, which is why the color shift happens almost instantly once cell walls fail. Spear thickness changes the conductive path length from surface to core, which is why the window spans from two minutes for pencil-thin stalks to four for thick ones — the water stays at a fixed 212°F throughout, so only distance and time change. Heavy salting also speeds early membrane breakdown at the surface.',
