    technicalDeepDive: 'Steel-cut oats are cracked groats with an intact bran layer, so water has to work through that fibrous shell before reaching the starchy endosperm, which is why most of the simmer window goes to hydration rather than to near-instant gelatinization the way refined flour would show. Holding the pot at a bare 200°F simmer rather than a rolling boil keeps convection gentle enough that starch granules swell and release amylose gradually instead of rupturing all at once, which produces a creamy suspension rather than a broken, watery one. That gradual amylose release is also what thickens the liquid around the grains into a cohesive matrix rather than loose water around intact kernels.',
