    technicalDeepDive: 'Green and French lentils keep an intact seed coat, unlike hulled and split varieties, and that coat slows how fast water can diffuse into the starchy cotyledon inside, which is why this lentil holds its outline through a 20-to-25-minute simmer that would blow a split red lentil apart into mush. Covering the pot traps enough steam to hold a steady simmer with minimal evaporative heat loss, so the cooking rate stays consistent rather than drifting as liquid evaporates. Inside the intact coat, starch in the cotyledon absorbs water and gelatinizes progressively from the outside in, and the coat\'s own pectin-rich structure keeps the lentil cohesive even once the interior has fully softened.',
