    technicalDeepDive: 'Cutting the chuck to 1.5-inch cubes multiplies surface area, so a properly loaded batch sears hot enough for myosin and actin to coagulate and brown within moments, while a crowded pot can\'t recover fast enough to outrun the moisture the meat sheds. Once submerged, heat transfer shifts from direct conduction to convection through the braising liquid, penetrating a piece this small far faster than a whole roast. Chuck\'s dense collagen, built for constant shoulder movement, hydrolyzes into gelatin only gradually as heat holds near 195-200°F for hours, which is why a cube still chewy at two hours needs more time at temperature, not less.',
