      'Use a Dutch oven that fits the roast snugly — if there is more than 2 inches of clearance on all sides, the liquid spreads and the roast cooks more by braising and less by the dry-heat exposure that creates the crust. A 5-6 quart Dutch oven works for most 3-4 lb roasts; avoid an 8-quart if your roast is smaller.',
      'If your Dutch oven lid does not seal tightly, place a sheet of parchment paper between the pot rim and lid before closing it. This creates a temporary seal that reduces liquid evaporation. Without a tight seal, you may lose 2-3 cups of liquid over 3-3.5 hours, and the roast will partially braise and partially roast in a drier environment.',
