    technicalDeepDive: 'A whole 3-4 lb roast has several times the radius of a stew cube, so reaching the same 195-203°F endpoint takes hours longer at the centre even though the oven itself stays at 325°F: heat works inward by conduction from a seared exterior that finished converting long before the core caught up. That radial lag also explains the tough middle stage, where fibers partway to the centre have tightened from coagulation but their local connective tissue hasn\'t yet had time to soften and re-moisten them. Only the bottom half sits in braising liquid, so the exposed top relies on convected heat rather than direct liquid contact, which is the physical reason it needs turning partway through.',
