    technicalDeepDive: 'The flat is a worked pectoral muscle laced with collagen, and collagen only liquefies into gelatin well above the point where muscle proteins have already coagulated and squeezed out moisture — pulling it at the 145°F safe minimum would leave it dry and shear-resistant rather than sliceable. Because the pot stays covered, the braising liquid keeps steady conductive contact with the meat instead of losing energy to open-air evaporation, so four to five hours at 300°F amounts to slow poaching rather than roasting. That gentler, liquid-mediated heat gives the connective tissue time to break down without the muscle fibers racing ahead of it and seizing up first.',
