      'Water coverage \'by 2 inches\' is intentional — too shallow and bones break the surface as liquid reduces; too deep and the stock becomes so dilute that gelatin concentration never reaches the gel-setting threshold, no matter how long you cook.',
      'The lid-ajar gap is about 1/4-inch — sealed fully, pressure builds and steam condenses back into the pot, diluting the stock; left fully open, evaporation happens too fast and reduces the gelatin concentration unevenly. The narrow gap lets steam escape at a controlled rate.',
      'A Dutch oven\'s thick walls and bottom maintain even 200°F without hot spots or sudden temperature swings — a thin aluminum or stainless pot tends to develop localized hotter zones that can push the simmer into a boil in patches, which is the opposite of what you want.',
