    technicalDeepDive: 'There\'s no internal temperature to chase here because the bones and cartilage are the ingredient, not a piece of meat being brought to a target — the goal is to hydrolyze collagen out of connective tissue into the water as dissolved gelatin, a process driven by sustained time near a simmer rather than any single threshold, which is what lets the cooled liquid set into a gel. Four to six hours gives that slow hydrolysis time to pull gelatin from tougher joint and neck cartilage a shorter cook would leave un-rendered. Roasting the bones beforehand adds a separate layer of chemistry, driving off surface moisture and browning proteins through Maillard reactions before they ever meet water.',
