      'Scoring the skin requires only a sharp chef\'s knife cutting through the skin layer in a tight cross-hatch (about 1/4-inch spacing), but not into the fat underneath — deep scores release fat too early and unevenly. The scores serve as relief valves, letting fat underneath render without the skin shrinking or puffing unevenly.',
      'Searing happens in a hot, dry Dutch oven with no added oil — pork belly is fatty enough to brown itself. Work in batches so each cube touches the pan bottom directly rather than steaming on top of others. Brown on multiple faces (don\'t flip constantly, but do turn each cube once or twice) to build fond on all sides.',
      'The braising liquid should come 3/4 of the way up the sides of the cubes — deep enough to submerge the bottom faces and conduct heat upward, but shallow enough that the top surfaces are exposed to steam and dry heat. This dual-zone cooking ensures the skin translates properly while the meat below poaches gently.',
      'The Dutch oven\'s flat, heavy bottom distributes heat evenly so no single zone of the pot gets hotter than another — critical because pork belly\'s high fat content means scorching is a real risk in thin-bottomed pots. The mass of the pot also acts as a heat buffer, preventing temperature spikes when you open the lid to check progress.',
