    technicalDeepDive: 'Bread crust browning and cheese melting run on very different timelines, and this cook is built around that mismatch. Butterfat begins to break down at a temperature well below the point where bread\'s surface starches and sugars start browning, so holding the pan near 275°F keeps that crust reaction slow enough for heat to finish moving all the way through the crumb and fully liquefy the cheddar\'s fat and protein matrix before the surface darkens past caramel. Cheddar\'s tighter protein network also needs more time at gentle heat to relax and flow than a softer cheese would, which is part of why this sandwich needs minutes rather than the seconds a thinner filling might take.',
