    technicalDeepDive: 'Egg white and yolk are separate protein systems that firm at different points on the heat curve; albumin in the white sets well before yolk proteins do, which is exactly why sunny-side up works: low, steady heat gives the white time to fully coagulate while the yolk stays well short of setting. Egg white also conducts heat poorly relative to its thickness, so a hotter pan would firm and brown the bottom before that heat finishes moving up through to the dome\'s top. Keeping the butter at a foam rather than a brown keeps the fat well under the point where it would start contributing bitter compounds of its own.',
