    technicalDeepDive: 'The dark crust in blackening forms because ground paprika and other spices in the rub char and caramelize at a much lower point than the fish flesh underneath would scorch, and the butter dip gives those spices a fat medium to adhere to and conduct heat through quickly enough to build that crust within the couple of minutes each side gets. Cod itself has very little connective tissue holding its muscle segments together, so once heat conducts past that dark shell, the lean flesh cooks through fast, reaching a flaky 145°F in roughly the same window the crust needs to fully form. That close match between how quickly the spices char and how quickly a thin, lean fillet cooks through is what keeps the technique from either undercooking the center or burning the crust to ash.',
