    proTip: 'Do not crack eggs directly over a hot skillet — the violent thermal shock spreads thin watery whites everywhere and risks broken yolks. Crack them into a small ramekin first, then gently slide them into foaming butter. Add 1 tsp of water and snap a tight glass lid on for 90 seconds to steam-set the tricky top albumen without overcooking the delicate yolk.',
    verificationBasis: 'American Egg Board / Egg Safety Center stovetop egg preparation standards (albumen sets at 144°F–149°F, yolk thickens at 158°F); Serious Eats "The Science of Sunny-Side Up Eggs" steam-cover method; no probe used — internalTempTargetF omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Egg white and yolk are different proteins with different coagulation points, and a fully set white around a still-liquid yolk depends on that gap: albumen firms at a meaningfully lower temperature than the proteins in yolk, so gentle, steady heat gives the white time to fully set while the yolk, cushioned by its own fat and slower to heat through, stays well below its own setting point. A hotter pan would set the white\'s outer layer almost instantly but push its edge temperature high enough to firm the yolk\'s rim before the center ever warms. Basting with foaming butter adds a gentler second heat path across the top without needing to flip the egg.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook sunny side up eggs', 'skillet sunny side up eggs time', 'how to make sunny side up eggs not runny white', 'stove temperature for sunny side up eggs', 'perfect fried eggs runny yolk'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook sunny-side up eggs in a skillet: 3–4 min over medium-low. The 1-tsp water lid trick to set whites perfectly.',
