    proTip: 'Skip the pot of boiling water entirely: boiling packaged gnocchi often turns them into gummy, waterlogged starch balls. Sautéing them raw directly in hot butter browns the potato starches through the Maillard reaction, creating a crisp tater-tot-like shell with a fluffy cloud center.',
    verificationBasis: 'De Cecco & Giovanni Rana direct pan-sauté guidelines for fresh/packaged gnocchi; Bon Appétit crispy skillet gnocchi method; no meat probe required — internalTempTargetF omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Frying gnocchi directly relies on conduction from the dry pan to drive off surface moisture and caramelize the exposed potato starch, a heat-transfer mode very different from the convection a pot of simmering water would use on the same dumpling. Because the exterior browns in dry fat rather than sitting in water, the surface starch dehydrates and browns instead of simply swelling and softening, while the moisture sealed inside each piece has nowhere to go once that shell sets, so it keeps gelatinizing the interior starch by trapped steam even as the outside crisps. A single uncrowded layer keeps every piece in direct metal contact, since that conductive contact is what drives the browning.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook gnocchi in skillet', 'pan fried gnocchi no boil time', 'skillet crispy gnocchi recipe', 'how to cook packaged gnocchi in pan', 'crispy pan seared gnocchi'],
