    technicalDeepDive: 'Dried semolina pasta needs water and heat above its starch\'s gelatinization threshold to soften, and a sealed chamber near 239°F forces water into that dense matrix faster than an open pot at 212°F, which is how four minutes under pressure replaces what is normally a much longer boil. The immediate quick release matters as much as the cook time: venting right as the timer ends stops starch conversion at that point, whereas a natural release would keep driving heat and water inward and push the pasta past tender into mush. Off the heat, evaporated milk\'s concentrated proteins help keep melted fat from three different cheeses suspended as a smooth emulsion instead of separating into an oily, broken sauce.',
