    technicalDeepDive: 'Quinoa is technically a seed, wrapped in a coat far thinner than the bran layer on a grain like rice, which is why it hydrates and gelatinizes so quickly once heat and water reach it. The white spiral on each cooked seed is the germ, which absorbs water and swells at a different rate than the surrounding starchy tissue, and that mismatch in expansion is what physically pushes the germ outward into its curl. Because the seed coat offers so little resistance, only a brief burst of active pressure is needed, and the 1:1.25 water ratio is calibrated so the seed\'s own volume of liquid is fully absorbed rather than pooling once cooking ends.',
