    proTip: 'Always deglaze the bottom of the stainless inner pot thoroughly after sautéing the lamb and onions before locking the lid. Any browned spices or meat fond stuck to the base will insulate the thermal sensor and trigger a frustrating "BURN" error 5 minutes into the pressure cycle.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Lamb from Farm to Table" safe cooking standards (145°F minimum; braising cuts cooked to 195°F+ for tenderness); Instant Pot Meat/Stew Program guidelines (25–30 min for 1.5-inch lamb cubes); America\'s Test Kitchen Pressure Cooker Curry testing.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Shoulder is a heavily worked muscle laced with collagen, the protein that keeps an unbraised cut chewy no matter how precisely it hits a safe internal temperature. The USDA\'s 145°F threshold is a safety minimum, not a texture target, and collagen here does not meaningfully convert to gelatin until well beyond that point, which is why the target runs up to 205°F. Sealed pressure raises the liquid\'s boiling point to roughly 239°F, speeding that hydrolysis enough to collapse tough shoulder in under thirty minutes rather than the several hours an open braise at 212°F would need. Mild acid in the tomato base helps the same breakdown along while carrying browned fond from the sear into the sauce.',
