    technicalDeepDive: 'Shoulder muscle carries both heavy collagen and more intramuscular fat than a leaner cut like the brisket flat, so chuck converts to gelatin over roughly the same range while staying richer and less prone to drying out even at 200–210°F. Two-inch cubes are considerably larger than the thin cross-cuts used for osso buco, so heat has farther to travel by conduction from the wine-and-stock liquid to the center of each piece, part of why this braise runs longer than the veal shanks despite a similar oven temperature. Searing in rendered bacon fat rather than neutral oil adds its own Maillard compounds to the fond before the liquid goes in.',
