    technicalDeepDive: 'Point-cut brisket carries more intramuscular fat and connective tissue than the flat, which is why it holds up to hours at 200°F–210°F without drying out the way a leaner cut would. Held under a bare simmer rather than a boil, the cured fibers get time to slowly release collagen into the broth instead of seizing the way hard agitation would force them to. The curing brine\'s salt and nitrite also change how the proteins denature and hold onto moisture, part of why this brisket can carry to 195°F–205°F internal, well past where an uncured roast would already be dry, and still slice as a coherent, moist slab.',
