    technicalDeepDive: 'Beef cheeks are a constantly working facial muscle, so they carry more collagen per ounce than almost any other cut, and that density dictates both the low 300°F oven and the 3.5-to-4-hour hold: higher heat would firm and dry the exterior before the deep collagen network ever liquefied. The parchment cartouche resting on the braising liquid slows surface evaporation, keeping the liquid close enough to cover the cheeks so heat transfer stays even, moisture-mediated conduction rather than dry oven air. Reducing the wine by half before it touches the meat drives off its most volatile acidity, so the collagen breaks down over those hours without the sauce turning sharp.',
