    technicalDeepDive: 'Shrimp muscle is almost pure actomyosin with little connective tissue or intramuscular fat to slow heat penetration, so it moves through protein denaturation faster than almost anything else on the grill — a 500°F grate needs only a couple of minutes per side to cook it through. The curl is a direct mechanical signature of that denaturation: as fibers along the shrimp\'s inner curve seize and shorten, the body bends into a C, and the tighter the curl, the more those fibers have over-contracted and squeezed free water out. Because the flesh carries a high water content and almost no fat buffer, there is very little margin between the loose C-shaped optimum and the tightly curled, moisture-expelled overcooked state, leaving little room for error even at two to three minutes a side.',
