    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F safe minimum for whole beef cuts; 125°F pull for chef-standard medium-rare per Serious Eats\' Food Lab tenderloin testing and Cook\'s Illustrated grilled-filet method. Two-zone sear-then-indirect approach from Meathead Goldwyn\'s reverse-sear guide on AmazingRibs.com.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Tenderloin comes from a muscle that does very little work in the live animal, so its fibers were never toughened by activity and carry almost no connective tissue to break down — the entire cook is about protein coagulation, not collagen conversion, which is why even medium-rare only needs to hit 125°F. That same lack of exertion means almost no marbling either, and fat is normally what slows and spreads heat conduction through a steak; without it, two full inches of direct 500°F exposure would blacken the outer inch long before the center ever moved. Searing first for two minutes a side builds the crust while the core is still cold, then dropping to 350°F indirect lets heat carry the center up gradually from the surface inward, rather than continuing under direct heat that would overshoot the target before the middle ever catches up.',
