    technicalDeepDive: 'Asparagus has no muscle protein to denature — its texture is governed by pectin, the cement between plant cell walls, which needs sustained heat to break down enough for a spear to bend instead of snapping. At 450°F direct heat, the outer surface reaches caramelization and light Maillard territory fast enough to blister and char before the pectin deep inside has fully softened, which is part of why constant rolling matters: it spreads that surface heat evenly rather than committing one face to full char while the far side stays raw-crisp. The bright green color survives because the exposure is short — chlorophyll only degrades into the duller olive tones of pheophytin with far longer heat exposure than a five-to-eight-minute grill session provides.',
