    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'A grill grate is the wrong surface for smash burgers — the fat drops through the bars into the fire, so instead of frying in its own rendered beef fat the patty steams and never builds the lacy crust the technique is named for. A cast-iron griddle or a preheated 12-inch skillet on the grate turns the grill into a flat-top: same live-fire heat, all the fat stays under the patty. Smash only once, in the first 10 seconds; a second smash squeezes juice out of an already-set crust and the burger dries out.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 160°F safe minimum for all ground beef; 500–550°F griddle temperature and single-smash timing from J. Kenji López-Alt\'s smash-burger testing (Serious Eats) and Meathead Goldwyn\'s smash-burger technique piece on AmazingRibs.com. Cast-iron-on-grate method used to translate the flat-top standard to a home grill, as documented in Weber\'s Way to Grill Every Meal (Jamie Purviance) grill-griddle chapter.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A patty smashed to about a quarter inch has a far higher surface-area-to-mass ratio than a thick burger, so the fast conductive jump from a 500-550°F cast-iron surface carries the whole cross-section to the 160°F minimum within a couple of minutes rather than the longer coast a thick patty needs. Cast iron\'s thermal mass makes that possible without the surface stalling: a cold ball of chuck dropped onto thinner metal would drag the local contact temperature down and soften the sear, but iron\'s heat capacity holds close to its preheated temperature through contact, so browning starts within seconds. Ground beef also has no intact fiber structure left to toughen from overcooking the way whole muscle does, so heat that would ruin a thick steak just builds crust faster here.',
