    technicalDeepDive: 'At just over an inch thick, a lamb loin chop has enough surface area relative to its mass that a 500°F direct sear can drive both faces through the Maillard range in a few minutes, with conduction reaching the center almost as fast as the surface browns. That is why the indirect side of the two-zone setup functions only as a buffer here rather than a primary stage the way it does for a thick steak: with so little mass left over once the surface browns, a slightly larger chop can go from the 130°F pull point to overdone within a single extra flip. The T-shaped cross-section also carries two separate muscles finishing at slightly different points along that same short window, joined only by the bone between them.',
