    proTip: 'A ripe pineapple is already ~10% sugar by weight, so the grate needs to be spotless and oiled or the caramelising fructose welds the rings to the bars and tears them in half on the flip. Blotting the rings dry is the other half of the trick — surface water on the flesh has to evaporate before browning starts, and a wet ring greys before it grills. Cut rings, not spears; the ring shape lets the flat face of the fruit contact the grate for real grill marks instead of the four-line kiss a spear gets.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — pineapple is judged by grill-mark depth and edge translucency, so internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2, matching the corn, mushroom, and asparagus conventions in this dataset. Direct-heat time range and ring-vs-spear guidance from the Weber Grilling Guide grilled-fruit chart and Steven Raichlen\'s grilled-pineapple technique in How to Grill.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Pineapple flesh has essentially no protein to speak of, so what darkens on the grate is caramelization of the fruit\'s own sugars rather than a Maillard reaction, a different chemical pathway that runs on carbohydrate alone. Because that reaction accelerates once it gets going, the grill-mark pattern tends to go from faint to deep caramel within a fairly narrow window near the end of each side\'s cook rather than building up gradually and evenly like Maillard browning on a protein-rich food. The translucent look the edges take on is a separate process, pectin and cell wall structure breaking down under sustained heat, which is why a ring can look glassy and softened at the edge while the flat face is still only midway through forming its browned shell.',
