    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Ham and Food Safety" (fully smoked/cured ham hocks are pre-cooked and safe once heated through, so this cook is limited by legume tenderness, not meat temperature); USA Dry Pea & Lentil Council "Cooking Dry Peas" (split peas cook to full breakdown in 7–8 hrs in a slow cooker on LOW, unsoaked); Crock-Pot 6-Qt Recipe Collection "Split Pea Soup with Ham" (LOW 7–8 hrs). Internal-temp target omitted per HR-2 — doneness is a spoon-coat and pea-collapse test, not a probe.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Because the peas here are split rather than whole, they arrive with no seed coat standing between broth and the starchy cotyledon inside, so water and heat penetrate from the first hour instead of fighting through a hull the way whole beans do. That head start is why a pound of dry, unsoaked split peas can disintegrate into a smooth purée within one LOW cycle instead of needing the harder treatment tougher legumes demand. The hock\'s shank meat and connective tissue sit in that same simmering broth the whole time, slowly rendering fat and gelatin into the liquid the peas are thickening.',
