    proTip: 'Do not prick the casings — pricking is a leftover from grilling to prevent flare-ups, and in a slow cooker it just drains the fat and salt out into the sauce, giving you dry, hollow links surrounded by greasy marinara. Leave the casings intact and let each link poach in its own juices.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Sausage and Food Safety" — raw pork sausage safe minimum 160°F internal; Johnsonville "Slow Cooker Italian Sausage" published cooking guidance (LOW 4–6 hrs, do not prick); Crock-Pot 6-Qt recipe archive "Sausage and Peppers" (LOW 4–5 hrs); the no-prick rule is documented in Cook\'s Illustrated sausage-braising notes.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Keeping the casing intact matters because it\'s the only thing holding the link\'s rendered fat and juices inside as heat conducts in from the surrounding marinara; ground pork forcemeat has no muscle-fiber grain of its own to trap moisture, so once that fat liquefies, an open casing gives it nowhere to go but out. Half-submerging the links means the top cooks by contact with hot sauce and rising steam while the bottom braises directly in liquid, a split exposure that evens out over the hours since the insert itself holds a steady, uniform temperature. Peppers soften as their pectin-bound walls break down in that same warm, acidic bath.',
    keywords: ['how long to slow cook italian sausage', 'sausage and peppers slow cooker time', 'crockpot italian sausage', 'slow cooker sausage internal temp', 'sausage and peppers low all day'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook Italian sausage and peppers in the slow cooker: 4–5 hours on LOW to 160°F. Why pricking the casings ruins the links.',
