    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F + 3-min rest whole-cut pork safe minimum; National Pork Board "Pork Cuts Chart" (country-style ribs are cut from the shoulder-blade end and behave like small pork shoulder — pull-tender window 190–200°F internal); Crock-Pot 6-Qt Set & Forget cooking chart (boneless country-style pork ribs LOW 6–7 hrs); the sauce-thinning-with-stock method is documented in Cook\'s Illustrated "Slow-Cooker Barbecued Country-Style Pork Ribs".',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Country-style ribs come off the shoulder-blade end of the loin rather than the true shoulder, carrying meaningfully less connective tissue than a whole butt, which is why six to seven hours on LOW is enough here versus the eight-plus hours a shoulder roast needs to fully convert its collagen. The muscle fibers in this cut run in mixed directions where loin and shoulder muscle meet, so as connective tissue breaks down the meat doesn\'t pull into the long uniform strands a pure shoulder cut gives, instead splitting into shorter, looser pieces. The vinegar in the braise also nudges surface proteins toward earlier breakdown at the meat\'s edge, part of why this cut reaches pull-apart texture faster than a whole shoulder needs.',
