    technicalDeepDive: 'A thin coat of oil does real conductive work: it fills the microscopic gaps between the meat\'s surface and the pan that would otherwise trap insulating air, so heat transfers into the tenderloin faster and more evenly than a bare sear manages. Pork tenderloin is a whole, lean muscle with very little intramuscular fat, closer in composition to chicken breast than to a marbled chop, which is part of why the margin between properly cooked and dry is so narrow here. The pink band visible at the center on pull is myoglobin that hasn\'t fully denatured yet, and that pigment shift happens gradually across a range rather than at one sharp point, which is why the current whole-muscle pork standard allows a blush of color that an older, higher target would have cooked past.',
