      'Preheat the skillet dry (without the butter) for the full 10 minutes. If you add the butter at the start of the preheat, it will burn and smoke by minute 6-7, leaving burnt specks in the batter and a slightly rancid flavor. Add the butter in the last 30 seconds before pouring, just long enough for it to melt and coat the pan evenly without browning.',
      'The skillet\'s thermal mass is critical to the rise. A 10-inch cast-iron skillet weighs 3-4 pounds and retains enough heat to deliver the thermal shock that causes rapid steam expansion when cold batter hits the hot surface. A lighter ceramic or aluminum baking dish lacks this thermal reserve and will produce a sluggish, incomplete rise. An 8-inch cast-iron will work but produces a lower climb (2-3 inches instead of 3-4).',
      'Position the skillet on the center oven rack, not high or low. High racks expose the top to more radiant heat from the upper element, setting the top before the sides have time to climb. Low racks do the opposite — the sides climb but the top stays pale. Center rack is the compromise for even rise and browning.',
      'Do not use a glass-door oven if you can avoid it, or at minimum cover the window with a damp towel to prevent viewing. The temptation to peek in the first 15 minutes is enormous, and even a quick look disrupts the steam inside the oven. Glass-door seals are typically less airtight than solid-door ovens, compounding the problem.',
