    technicalDeepDive: 'Unlike a preheated skillet, starting from room temperature means the oven\'s convective and radiant heat does most of the early work, warming the dough from the top and sides while the iron itself only slowly catches up to the surrounding air. That slow catch-up matters for a dough this thick: butter has time to melt and redistribute through the flour and sugar before any single layer sets, rather than flash-melting against a hot surface and spreading the dough thin before structure forms. Sugar and egg proteins begin to set once the local temperature crosses the same starch-gelatinization window any quickbread needs, but a mass this thick reaches that window at very different times at the edge versus the center, which is the real reason the target is an underdone core rather than uniform doneness.',
