    technicalDeepDive: 'Raw potato shreds are opaque white because their starch granules are tightly packed and crystalline; as they absorb heat, those granules swell and gelatinize, which turns the interior translucent and binds the mass into one cohesive cake instead of loose strands. That gelatinized starch matrix, not the crust alone, is what lets the whole disc get flipped in one piece rather than falling apart. Cast iron\'s mass matters the moment the cold, water-laden shreds go in: a thin pan would lose enough surface heat to drop below the point where browning starts, while cast iron\'s reserve keeps the surface close to its preheated temperature so the crust begins forming immediately.',
