    technicalDeepDive: 'Fish muscle is bundled into short segments called myotomes, separated by thin collagen sheets, and it is those sheets softening with heat, not the muscle fibers themselves, that let the fillet flake cleanly along its natural seams. Halibut carries very little intramuscular fat, so once its proteins coagulate and squeeze out moisture there is no fat reserve left to keep the fiber bundles lubricated, and the same few degrees that would only firm up a fattier fish leave halibut dry and cottony instead. Direct pan contact sets the exterior almost immediately, but the fillet\'s modest thickness means the core keeps climbing toward that narrow target well after it leaves the heat.',
