    donenessCue: 'Every visible grain of rice has fully absorbed the broth with no puddled liquid in the bottom of the pot; a Thermapen driven into the top of the thickest thigh reads 165°F+; the rice fluffs into distinct grains when raked with a fork rather than clumping.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'The pot-in-pot instinct is wrong here — the thighs sit directly ON the rice. The rendered chicken fat and juices drip through the rice as it cooks and season it from the top down, which is the whole reason people cook this dish in one pot instead of two. If you set the thighs on the trivet, you get plain steamed rice and separately steamed chicken, which is a different (worse) dish.',
    verificationBasis: 'America\'s Test Kitchen "Multicooker Perfection" (2017) One-Pot Chicken and Rice chapter, 10-minute HP + 10-min NPR benchmark for 1.5 cups white rice with 1.5 lb boneless thighs on top; Instant Brands Official Rice Program guidelines (10 min HP for long-grain white rice, 1:1.3 ratio); USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Rice starch cannot gelatinize until it has both free water and enough heat, and a sealed chamber near 239°F supplies both faster than an open pot, which is why 1.5 cups of rinsed long-grain rice fully hydrates and sets in just ten minutes, far faster than the same rice would take in an open saucepan at atmospheric pressure. The thigh meat sitting on top adds thermal mass without adding meaningfully to the cook time, since chicken thigh\'s own protein denaturation completes well within the same ten-minute window the rice needs to fully gelatinize. The full natural release afterward matters because residual pressure keeps driving conductive heat through both layers even after the heating element shuts off, which is what lets the thigh\'s center finish climbing to 165°F without the rice scorching against the pot floor.',
