    verificationBasis: 'Serious Eats "The Best Pressure Cooker Bolognese Sauce" (Daniel Gritzer, 2017 — 30-minute HP + 15-min NPR benchmark; wine-then-milk reduction sequence for classical Bolognese emulsion); USDA FSIS "Ground Beef and Food Safety" 160°F safe minimum for ground meat (readily exceeded during the 30-min pressurised braise at 212°F chamber temperature); probe verification with ThermoWorks Thermapen One.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The browning step on Sauté does different chemistry than everything that follows. Dry contact with the hot pot bottom pushes the meat\'s surface into the roughly 280 to 330°F Maillard range, the only point in this recipe surface browning can happen, since everything after the lid locks is submerged in tomato and wine. Once sealed, the elevated pressure chamber works toward a slower goal: collagen woven through even ground beef and pork keeps hydrolyzing into gelatin through the full thirty-minute hold, which is what gives a pressure-cooked Bolognese its clingy, silky body rather than just cooked meat suspended in tomato.',
