    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum for poultry; Serious Eats / J. Kenji López-Alt "One-Pot Pasta" method (pasta cooked directly in seasoned dairy liquid at ~1:2 pasta-to-liquid ratio); Le Creuset 12-inch signature skillet stovetop cookware guide (medium-high 375°F sear range); ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The dish moves through two heat-transfer modes in one pan: searing the chicken is direct conduction from hot metal, browning the exterior into the Maillard range within minutes, while adding broth and cream shifts the pan to convective heat through a simmering liquid that, being mostly water, can\'t climb much past water\'s own boiling point no matter how high the burner runs. That capped, gentler heat lets the dry pasta finish cooking by convection alone, its starch granules swelling and gelatinizing gradually rather than being blasted by the rolling boil a big pasta pot would use. Pulling the chicken out beforehand keeps its already-finished proteins from sitting in that cooler simmer any longer than necessary.',
