    food: 'Chicken Piccata (Skillet, Lemon-Caper Pan Sauce)',
    foodSlug: 'chicken-piccata-skillet',
    appliance: 'skillet',
    cutOrPrep: '4 boneless skinless chicken breasts butterflied and pounded to 1/4-inch cutlets (8 pieces total), seasoned with salt/pepper, dredged lightly in flour and shaken off; sautéed in 2 tbsp olive oil + 2 tbsp butter in a 12-inch stainless skillet in 2 batches; finished with 1/2 cup dry white wine, 1/2 cup chicken broth, 1/4 cup fresh lemon juice, 3 tbsp capers, and 3 tbsp cold butter swirled off-heat',
    state: 'fresh',
    tempF: 375,
    tempC: 191,
    tempFormatted: 'Medium-high heat (375°F–400°F surface, butter foam actively bubbling without browning at the start)',
    timeMinMinutes: 10,
    timeMaxMinutes: 14,
    timeFormatted: '10–14 mins total (5–7 min in 2 batches for cutlets + 5–7 min pan sauce reduction)',
    flipAtMinutes: 2,
    internalTempTargetF: 165,
    internalTempTargetFormatted: '165°F (74°C) — USDA FSIS chicken minimum, hit almost instantly on 1/4-inch cutlets',
    restMinutes: 3,
    donenessCue: 'Cutlets are golden-brown on both sides with a light dusting of crisped flour; pan sauce has reduced by half, coats the back of a spoon, and is a pale straw-yellow (not oily-broken); capers have plumped and released their brine into the sauce; final butter swirl gives a glossy, emulsified finish.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Pound the cutlets thin (1/4-inch) and dredge in flour — the flour does two jobs: it protects the delicate chicken from overcooking and it thickens the pan sauce when it dissolves. Add the final 3 tbsp of cold butter OFF the heat, swirling the pan constantly; if the pan is still hot enough to sizzle, the butter breaks into greasy oil instead of the silky emulsion piccata is famous for. Use fresh lemon juice only — bottled tastes flat and adds a metallic edge.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum; Marcella Hazan "Essentials of Classic Italian Cooking" scaloppine al limone technique; Cook\'s Illustrated "Chicken Piccata" (pound-dredge-batch-sear-mount-butter sequence); All-Clad D3 stainless 12-inch skillet stovetop cooking guide (medium-high 375°F–400°F for cutlet sauté); ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'At a quarter inch, these cutlets are thin enough that conductive heat from the 375 to 400°F pan reaches the core almost as fast as it browns the surface, leaving little window for the interior to overshoot 165°F while the crust develops. Wine deglazes the fond partly because its acidity helps dissolve the polymerized browned compounds stuck to the pan, and because alcohol\'s lower boiling point than water means much of it flashes off quickly, concentrating flavor without diluting the sauce for long. Butter added once the pan is off direct heat melts gradually enough that its fat disperses as countless tiny droplets suspended through the liquid rather than pooling into a single greasy layer, which is the basic physics behind any mounted sauce.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook chicken piccata in a skillet', 'chicken piccata cook time', 'chicken cutlet pan sauce time', 'stovetop chicken piccata internal temp', 'lemon caper chicken skillet'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook chicken piccata in a skillet: 10–14 min total. Pound to 1/4-inch, sear in 2 batches to 165°F, mount butter off-heat.',
