    appliance: 'skillet',
    cutOrPrep: '4 boneless skinless chicken breasts butterflied and pounded to 1/2-inch (8 cutlets), seasoned and dredged lightly in flour; seared in 2 tbsp olive oil + 2 tbsp butter in a 12-inch stainless skillet in 2 batches; sauce built in the same pan with 10 oz sliced cremini or baby bella mushrooms, 3 minced garlic cloves, 3/4 cup dry Marsala wine (never sweet), 3/4 cup chicken broth, 1/2 cup heavy cream, and 2 tbsp cold butter to finish',
    state: 'fresh',
    tempF: 400,
    tempC: 204,
    tempFormatted: 'Medium-high sear (400°F surface) then medium simmer (205°F) for the sauce build',
    timeMinMinutes: 20,
    timeMaxMinutes: 25,
    timeFormatted: '20–25 mins total (8 min for 2 cutlet batches + 6 min mushroom sauté + 6–10 min sauce reduction and chicken re-warm)',
    flipAtMinutes: 3,
    internalTempTargetF: 165,
    internalTempTargetFormatted: '165°F (74°C) — USDA FSIS chicken minimum, hit in first pass on 1/2-inch cutlets, verified again after the sauce reheat',
    restMinutes: 3,
    donenessCue: 'Mushrooms are deeply browned and have released and reabsorbed their liquid twice (a sign of proper high-heat sauté, not a steamed simmer); Marsala reduction has thickened to a light nappe (coats a spoon and drips slowly); sauce is a warm caramel-brown with visible ribbons of cream; cutlets read 165°F on a Thermapen when reheated in the sauce.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Use DRY Marsala (marked "secco" on Italian labels), never sweet Marsala — sweet Marsala is a dessert wine and will give you a cloying cough-syrup sauce. Sear the mushrooms in a HOT dry pan without stirring for 3 minutes before adding garlic; crowding or stirring too early releases their water and steams them into slimy gray bits instead of browning them. Reduce the Marsala until it is a syrupy glaze BEFORE the cream goes in, or the sauce will taste boozy and thin.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum; Marcella Hazan "The Classic Italian Cook Book" pollo al marsala technique (dry Marsala only, mushroom pre-browning); Cook\'s Illustrated "Chicken Marsala" (Marsala reduction to glaze before cream); All-Clad D3 stainless 12-inch skillet stovetop cooking guide (medium-high 400°F for cutlet sauté); ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Mushrooms are mostly water by weight, so when they first hit the hot dry pan that moisture has to boil off before the surface can climb past 212°F into Maillard range, which is why a crowded or stirred pan steams them instead of browning them. Reducing the Marsala until syrupy concentrates its residual sugars and flavor compounds through simple evaporation, the same mechanism that turns any thin liquid into a glaze. Doing that reduction before the cream goes in matters chemically too: cream\'s fat content is high enough to coat and protect its proteins in a hot, acidic liquid, but adding it while the sauce is still thin and full of unreduced acid gives those proteins less protection, raising the odds the dairy curdles instead of emulsifying smoothly.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook chicken marsala in a skillet', 'chicken marsala cook time', 'stovetop chicken marsala internal temp', 'dry marsala chicken pan sauce', 'mushroom marsala skillet chicken'],
