    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Slow-cooker eggs beat stovetop for meal prep because you set-and-forget 12 eggs at once and they finish with the crock still warm, waiting for you to peel when convenient. Downside: 3-hour cook time is way slower than 12 min stovetop. Use only when time isn\'t the constraint.',
    verificationBasis: 'FDA Food Code 145°F minimum for cooked eggs (well exceeded during 2.5-hr slow-cook); technique verified per Instant Pot Community forums and Crock-Pot 6-qt recipe archive. No probe target because eggs are set by time, not temp — internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Steam-poaching without direct water contact relies on a different heat-transfer mechanism than boiling or dry convection: water vapor condensing on the cool eggshell releases its latent heat of vaporization directly onto the shell surface, a far more efficient way to move energy into the egg than hot air alone. White proteins and yolk proteins coagulate at different points along that slow climb, with the white setting first and the yolk following, which is why a properly timed egg yields a fully firm white around a yolk set all the way through rather than one lagging behind the other. Because the whole two-and-a-half-to-three-hour window sits well above the FDA\'s cooked-egg minimum, the long cook is about texture, not safety.',
