    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 160°F ground meat safe minimum internal temperature (non-negotiable for beef/pork mixtures); smoking meatloaf profile per Steven Raichlen "Barbecue Bible" and Weber Smokey Mountain Cooking Guide. Tested with ThermoWorks Smoke X2 for probe accuracy.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Shaped as a dense 4x9-inch block rather than individual spheres, this loaf offers comparatively little surface for its overall mass, so heat has a long path to conduct inward before the center clears 160°F, which is why the cook stretches toward three hours at the same 250°F many quicker-cooking smoked proteins use. The panade of panko soaked in milk plays a specific role: swollen starch worked between the meat\'s protein strands during mixing limits how tightly those strands bond as they coagulate, keeping a dense loaf held at temperature this long from turning rubbery. Smoke\'s nitric oxide penetrates only a shallow band from the exposed surfaces, so the ring traced inside follows the loaf\'s silhouette rather than reaching a buried center the way it would in a smaller piece of meat.',
    keywords: ['smoked meatloaf time', 'how long to smoke a meatloaf', 'meatloaf smoker temperature', 'meatloaf internal temp 160', 'when to glaze meatloaf'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to smoke meatloaf: 2.5–3 hours at 250°F to 160°F internal. Why the glaze goes on in the final 15 minutes, not the first.',
