    proTip: 'The rubbery-skin problem on smoked chicken thighs is entirely a temperature issue — anything below 275°F leaves the sub-skin fat unrendered and the collagen unmelted, giving you the classic "chicken tar" texture. Smoke at 275°F, not 225°F, and the skin renders and crisps by the time the meat hits temp.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 165°F poultry safe minimum (thighs pulled at 175°F+ for connective tissue rendering — safe factor of ~10°F above minimum); skin-crisping requirement per Meathead Goldwyn "Amazing Ribs" chicken guide (below 275°F, skin renders too slowly). Tested on Traeger Pro 34 with cherry pellets.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The 12-hour uncovered dry brine does two things before these thighs reach the smoker: dissolved salt draws moisture to the surface and is partly reabsorbed by the muscle, seasoning it throughout, while open fridge air evaporates that surface water so skin starts the cook already dehydrated. That matters because moisture-loaded skin has to boil off water as latent heat before its own temperature can climb, so starting dry lets it reach browning temperatures sooner. Bone-in thighs also cook unevenly around the bone, since bone conducts heat more slowly than surrounding muscle, which is part of why the target sits well above the flat poultry minimum: meat pressed against the bone needs to clear a safe temperature too, not just the thicker exterior muscle.',
    keywords: ['smoked chicken thighs time', 'how long to smoke chicken thighs', 'chicken thighs 275 smoker', 'crispy skin smoked chicken', 'chicken thigh internal temp smoker'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to smoke bone-in chicken thighs: 75–90 minutes at 275°F to 175°F. Why 275°F beats 225°F for rendered, crisp skin.',
