    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Tri-tip has TWO grain directions in a single roast that meet at a 45° angle roughly two-thirds of the way through. Slice one section, rotate the roast, and slice the other — cutting the whole roast in one direction leaves half your slices with-the-grain and chewy. This is the "tri-tip" idiosyncrasy that most home cooks miss.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F whole-cut beef safe minimum; 125°F pull for smoker phase per Susie Bulloch "Hey Grill Hey" tri-tip guide (allows 10°F rise during 4–6 min sear phase); Santa Maria BBQ tradition dating to Central California ranch cooking documented in California Beef Council "Tri-Tip History" reference.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The reverse-sear sequence solves a problem a single-temperature cook cannot: cooking low and slow to 125°F first lets heat diffuse gradually inward, so by the time the roast leaves the smoker its internal gradient is nearly flat, with almost no gray, overcooked band under the crust. The brief high-heat sear only needs to push the outer fraction of an inch through the roughly 280-330°F Maillard range fast enough to brown before that same heat travels deep enough to overcook the interior, which is why the sear runs minutes rather than tens of minutes. Because the roast is already close to its target when searing begins, the carryover rise during the rest is small and predictable, landing it in the medium-rare range without guesswork.',
    keywords: ['smoked tri tip time', 'how long to smoke tri tip', 'santa maria tri tip', 'tri tip internal temp smoker', 'reverse sear tri tip grain direction'],
