    bonusFaqs: [
      {
        q: 'Does the size or variety of russet matter for cooking time?',
        a: 'Idaho russets and any commercial russet variety are fairly uniform in starch content, so variety doesn\'t significantly change cooking time. Size is everything—a 6 oz russet will be tender at 40 minutes, while an 8.5 oz russet will still be dense at 45 minutes. Select ones as uniformly sized as possible.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Is a thermometer worth using, or should I rely on the fork method?',
        a: 'The fork is more reliable because doneness is primarily a texture question, not just a temperature question. A potato at 205°F with incomplete starch breakdown will feel gummy; one at 208°F with complete gelatinization will feel fluffy. Treat 205°F as the absolute minimum and check fork resistance before pulling.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I cook russets at a lower temperature for longer to make them fluffier?',
        a: 'Lower temperature won\'t make them fluffier—it just extends cooking time to 55-60 minutes without changing texture, because fluffiness depends on starch gelatinization at a fixed temperature (around 140°F). Longer cooking past complete gelatinization only dries them further, making them more mealy, not better.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Should I flip potatoes halfway through cooking?',
        a: 'No. Flipping disrupts the conductive heat gradient and creates inconsistency. The potato that spent 20 minutes bottom-side-up has a slightly warm-through base, and flipping it resets that gradient and extends cook time.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why do some russets end up gummy instead of fluffy even when the fork slides through and internal temperature is right?',
        a: 'Older potatoes (stored for months) have lost moisture and the cell structure has degraded, making starch gelatinization uneven. Younger potatoes (fresh from harvest, fall/winter) hydrate more uniformly and gelatinize into a lighter texture. Mid-year purchases from storage facilities might need an extra 3-5 minutes to fully hydrate and break down starch.',
      },
    ],
    technicalDeepDive: 'A whole russet is dense enough that heat reaching the center depends almost entirely on conduction through the flesh rather than on the convective air swirling around it, which is why the cook time runs so much longer than for a cut vegetable. The internal target near 205 to 210°F is where starch granules and pectin in a mature potato fully hydrate and break down, producing a dry, fluffy interior instead of a gummy one. The oiled skin, meanwhile, sits directly in the stripped boundary layer of moving hot air, so it dehydrates and browns well ahead of the slow-conducting core beneath it.',
