    technicalDeepDive: 'A sausage casing is collagen, and collagen fibers shrink and tighten as they heat, which is the mechanism behind a well-cooked link\'s taut, blistered skin. Because the filling is ground meat, surface bacteria end up distributed throughout the matrix rather than confined to an exterior, so this record targets 160°F at the center under the USDA ground meat standard rather than the lower threshold used for a whole-muscle cut. The moderate 360°F setting matters for heat penetration: convection first has to carry energy through the casing, then conduct it through a dense cylinder of meat and fat, and a hotter setting would finish the exterior before that conduction reached the core.',
