    technicalDeepDive: 'Shrimp muscle is thin and packed almost entirely with myosin and actin, the same contractile proteins in any animal muscle, but with little connective tissue or fat to buffer the reaction, so coagulation happens fast and overshoots easily. As those proteins denature, fibers along the shrimp\'s inner curve shorten more than fibers on the outer curve, pulling the body into a curl; continued heat keeps tightening that same contraction well past the point of doneness. The short 5-7 minute window at 390°F reflects how little mass there is to heat through, since fast convective airflow reaches the shrimp\'s center almost as quickly as its exterior.',
