    technicalDeepDive: 'This one covers two phases in the same pan: an initial dry sear that browns the meat through Maillard reactions, then a simmer once the water and seasoning go in, at which point the added liquid clamps the pan\'s surface temperature near water\'s boiling point regardless of how much heat the iron still holds. That shift from dry to wet heat transfer is why no further browning develops once the seasoning stage begins; evaporation carries energy away as latent heat faster than the surface can climb back into browning range. The beef\'s interior, already safely cooked from searing, is simply maintained through the simmer while the dried spices rehydrate and dissolve into a thickening glaze as water evaporates off.',
