        q: 'Is cooking salmon skin-on actually necessary for this crispy-skin result, or is it optional?',
        a: 'Skin-on is not essential for salmon to cook safely, but it\'s crucial for this specific crispy-skin goal. The skin acts as an insulator, slowing how fast heat penetrates to the delicate flesh, and it gives you a wider window to develop a crust before overcooking the interior. Skinless salmon cooks much faster and requires precise heat control to avoid drying the flesh out.',
