    technicalDeepDive: 'Fish muscle is built differently from land-animal meat: its short fibers are separated by thin connective sheets that break down at markedly lower temperatures than beef or pork collagen, which is why salmon flesh is considered safely done at a noticeably lower temperature than red meat. Placing the fillet skin-down means the skin bears the brunt of the iron\'s stored heat first, rendering the fat layer beneath it and driving off surface moisture so it can brown and crisp, while acting as an insulating buffer that slows how fast heat reaches the delicate flesh above it. That buffering is what lets the long, undisturbed skin-side sear run through most of the cook without overcooking the flesh.',
