      'Drying scallops is as critical as the sear temperature itself. After removing the side muscle, lay them on paper towels in a single layer for 5–10 minutes so any trapped moisture evaporates from the crevices. Then pat gently with fresh paper towels immediately before the pan. Residual moisture on the surface will steam off instead of allowing Maillard browning, and steam = no crust.',
      'The pan must reach 450°F, confirmed either with an instant-read thermometer placed directly on the bare iron surface (away from where scallops will land) or the water-droplet test: a single drop of water should evaporate instantly (under 1 second) with an audible snap. If the drop beads up and takes 2–3 seconds to evaporate, the pan is only around 400°F and too cool for this cook.',
      'Oil choice is non-negotiable: use an oil with a smoke point well above 450°F (avocado oil, refined vegetable oil, canola, or grapeseed oil). Do not use butter or olive oil, which burn and turn bitter at this temperature. Pour just enough oil to coat the pan bottom (about 1 tablespoon for a 10-inch skillet), not a pool. Excess oil creates a film that slows heat transfer to the top and bottom faces of the scallop.',
      'Only two scallop surfaces will actually make contact with the iron: the flat top and flat bottom. The curved sides barely kiss the pan and won\'t develop a crust. Trying to rotate or roll scallops to \'sear all sides\' is wasted effort and disrupts the heat transfer to the critical faces.',
      'Scallop spacing is essential: they must not touch each other in the pan. When scallops sit close together, their combined cold mass cools the pan more significantly, slowing crust formation and creating steam pockets between them that boil the sides instead of searing them. Space them with at least 1/2-inch between each one; if you have more than 4–5 U/10 scallops or 6–7 U/15 scallops, cook them in two batches.',
