      'The sides of the scallop will transition from translucent and shiny (raw muscle protein) to milky, matte white (cooked protein). This color change happens over the 3–5-minute window and serves as a real-time progress indicator. Once all the visible side surface has turned opaque white, the interior is likely very close to 130°F.',
      'There should be a subtle, sweet caramel smell—the aroma of amino acids and sugars caramelizing through Maillard reactions. Any sharp, acrid, or burnt smell means the pan was too hot, the oil sat empty too long after preheating (causing oxidation), or the scallops have been on heat slightly too long. If you smell burnt rather than caramel, start fresh.',
      'A gentle fork-press test reveals texture: raw scallop muscle yields immediately under light pressure (soft, springy, like pressing a sponge). At 130°F (target), cooked scallop muscle feels slightly firm with a small amount of give—it won\'t be hard, but it won\'t indent easily like cold gelatin. This tactile change happens over just a few minutes and gives you a real-time doneness cue.',
