    technicalDeepDive: 'Potato gnocchi carries a moist, starchy dough that would gelatinize into a gummy paste if boiled first, so skipping that step keeps the raw starch granules on the surface intact until they meet the hot fat directly. Melted butter and olive oil together let the pan hold enough conductive heat to dehydrate that starch layer and drive it into a crisp shell, while the dumpling\'s interior, insulated by its own density, stays soft and steam-plumped. The milk solids in the butter brown alongside that starch layer, adding a nutty depth the oil alone would not produce.',
    keywords: ['cast iron pan crisped gnocchi time', 'how to cook gnocchi in cast iron without boiling', 'crispy gnocchi skillet temp'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to pan-crisp gnocchi in cast iron: 400°F (204°C) for 8–10 mins. No pre-boiling needed for golden crackling skins.',
