    technicalDeepDive: 'Cutting the squash into uniform 1-inch cubes before it touches the pot means every piece can go straight into water that\'s already at a full boil, which works because the small cube size keeps the distance heat has to travel to each cube\'s center short no matter the starting water temperature. Squash flesh carries a moderate starch and sugar content held in a comparatively loose cell structure, so its pectin breaks down and its starch granules swell quickly under sustained heat. Because the pieces are already peeled and seeded, there\'s no skin acting as a barrier to slow that process, which is part of why cubes this size gelatinize evenly all the way through rather than staying firmer at the core.',
