    technicalDeepDive: 'Brown rice keeps its bran and germ layers intact, and that fibrous coat is far less permeable than milled white rice, so water needs sustained contact at a full boil to reach the starchy endosperm underneath. Cooking in a large excess of water rather than a fixed ratio lets starch gelatinize while suspended in nearly unlimited liquid, so the amylose that leaches out during cooking drains away with the water instead of collecting around the grains. The long cook time relative to white rice reflects how much that bran layer delays heat and moisture penetration to the core, not any real difference in the starch granules themselves.',
    keywords: ['how long to boil brown rice', 'boiled brown rice pasta method time', 'cooking brown rice in boiling water'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to boil brown rice: boil pasta-style at 212°F (100°C) for 30–35 mins, drain, and rest 10 mins for fluffy, separate grains.',
