    technicalDeepDive: 'Breast muscle carries almost no intramuscular fat and negligible connective tissue, so its cooking window is narrow: myosin denatures near 140°F and actin tightens further as the muscle approaches 160°F, squeezing out moisture with every additional degree past that point. Because there is no collagen converting to gelatin to compensate, the 165°F endpoint has to be hit and then left alone. Searing first builds a Maillard crust on dry exterior protein before the marsala and mushroom liquid goes in; once the lid closes, the trapped steam keeps humidity high around the meat so the covered braise finishes cooking the center through gentle convection rather than continued surface drying.',
