      'Removing the back membrane is non-negotiable. It\'s a thin, papery layer on the concave side of the rack and acts as a physical barrier to liquid penetration. Slide a butter knife or your fingernail under one edge at the end of the rack, grip it with a paper towel for traction, and peel it off in one motion. If you leave it on, the cider and BBQ sauce will never fully penetrate that side of the ribs, and the meat-to-bone interface will stay grainy instead of softening to gelatin.',
      'Cutting the full rack into 3-4 bone sections increases the surface area significantly. Each cut exposes new meat that wasn\'t in contact with the braising liquid. Plan your cutting so sections are roughly 4-5 bones each; too many small pieces (2-bone sections) will dry out faster and won\'t build enough collagen-to-gelatin conversion in the time allowed.',
      'The coiling arrangement is specific: stand the rib sections upright around the perimeter of a round Dutch oven so each section has direct contact with the pot\'s sidewall. The cast iron conducts heat radially inward, so the perimeter ribs get the most even heat exposure. Interior spaces get gentle convection. This geometry is why a round pot works better than an oval for this recipe.',
