    technicalDeepDive: 'Rib meat sits over thin, well-exercised intercostal muscle laced with collagen that doesn\'t meaningfully convert into gelatin until it spends sustained time well above the temperature where the muscle fibers themselves are already fully set, which is why the target here runs to 195-200°F instead of stopping at a bare safety number. Keeping the oven at a comparatively low 300°F slows that conversion just enough that the meat doesn\'t lose moisture faster than the collagen can break down and replace it. The tightly covered pot holds cider vapor against the ribs throughout, so the connective tissue is softening in a saturated, humid environment rather than drying out under open convective heat the way an uncovered roast would.',
