    technicalDeepDive: 'Chuck comes from a heavily worked shoulder muscle dense with collagen, and the reason this stew\'s internal target sits near 200°F instead of stopping once the muscle fibers denature around 160°F is that the extra hour-plus at 325°F is entirely devoted to hydrolyzing that collagen into gelatin. That gelatin is what lets the beef stay moist rather than turn stringy at a temperature that would dry out a leaner cut, and it\'s also what gives the braising liquid its body once it dissolves in. Potato starch gelatinizes at a noticeably lower temperature than beef collagen needs to fully hydrolyze, which is the underlying reason this braise\'s vegetables and meat don\'t share the same cook window.',
