    technicalDeepDive: 'Left as one whole 3.5-pound roast rather than cut into stew-sized pieces, this chuck needs more time than a chopped braise for both heat and moisture to work their way to the center, and the fully submerged cooking liquid transfers that heat by direct conduction rather than the partial convection of a lid-trapped braise. The 205°F target sits above where a fork-tender braise would already be done because shredding demands collagen conversion to be essentially complete throughout the whole roast, not just softened at the edges, so the fibers separate cleanly along the grain instead of clinging to intact connective tissue. The dissolved gelatin and beef extractives that migrate into the cooking liquid over that time are what give the resulting jus its body, independent of any seasoning added at the end.',
