    technicalDeepDive: 'Because grinding redistributes any surface bacteria throughout the meat, USDA FSIS treats ground beef and pork as a single hazard zone and sets a flat 160°F target regardless of how rare an intact muscle cut from either animal could otherwise be. At a bare 200°F, the marinara sits at or just under a simmer, so each meatball\'s roughly 1.5-inch diameter absorbs heat through gentle conduction across the surrounding sauce rather than the more violent convection a hard boil would create, which matters because a matrix held together mainly by egg, breadcrumb, and rendered fat can break apart under aggressive agitation. That same modest sauce temperature also means the exterior never spikes hot enough to seize and squeeze out the moisture the panade is meant to retain.',
