    technicalDeepDive: 'Shrimp muscle is almost pure protein with very little insulating fat, so it moves from raw to overcooked over a narrow span of internal temperature, and holding the scampi liquid at a relatively low 225°F keeps the wine and butter from ever reaching a point where that window passes before the centers catch up. Because the flesh is so thin, heat conducts through to the middle almost as fast as it heats the surface, which is part of why a minute or two of extra time matters more here than it would with a thicker cut like a fish fillet. That same lack of fat means there is nothing to buffer the proteins once they pass their set point, so this low, gentle liquid temperature is doing the real work of keeping the shrimp tender rather than the short cook time alone.',
