    technicalDeepDive: 'Lobster meat is bound by a more delicate protein network than its shell would suggest, and that network firms and then quickly squeezes out moisture over just a few degrees, which is why poaching in fat rather than water matters: butter transfers heat more gently than boiling liquid and never subjects the meat to the convective turbulence that would toughen it. Clarifying the butter removes the milk solids that would otherwise scorch or break into a gritty emulsion at these temperatures, keeping the poaching medium smooth through the full cook. Poaching in fat instead of water also keeps the lobster\'s sweet glycogen and amino compounds from leaching outward the way they would if the tails were simmered directly in a pot of stock.',
