    technicalDeepDive: 'Starting from dry, unsoaked beans means water first has to migrate through the seed coat into each starch granule before heat can gelatinize anything, a slow diffusion process that accounts for most of the two-plus hours here rather than the cooking chemistry itself. Legume skins are held together by pectin and hemicellulose that need sustained time near a simmer to soften; too much heat too fast can gelatinize the interior starch before the skin has relaxed, causing it to split under pressure from the expanding center. The long simmer also breaks down complex sugars in the bean\'s cell walls that the human gut can\'t easily digest on its own, a separate process from starch gelatinization but one that runs on a similarly slow timeline.',
