    technicalDeepDive: 'A Dutch baby\'s rise is physics, not chemistry: there\'s no baking powder in the eggy batter, so the dramatic puff comes from water in the mixture flashing into steam against the screaming-hot buttered skillet, forcing the egg-and-flour matrix upward before it can collapse. Egg proteins begin coagulating well before the batter reaches the oven\'s full 425°F, so the outer layer firms into a stretchy skin fast enough to trap that expanding steam rather than let it vent away. The lactose and proteins in the batter then caramelize against both the hot pan below and the dry oven air above, driving the exposed batter into deep Maillard browning by the time the pancake finishes puffing.',
