    bonusFaqs: [
      {
        q: 'Can I cook a thinner ribeye (3/4-inch) or thicker one (1.25-inch) in the same time?',
        a: 'No. A 3/4-inch steak reaches 135°F in about 7–8 minutes and will be overdone if you cook it for 10; a 1.25-inch steak needs 13–15 minutes. The 1-inch thickness is ideal for air fryers because it balances crust development and interior temperature in a convenient window.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Should I put anything in the air fryer basket under the steak, like parchment paper or a drip tray?',
        a: 'Parchment reduces airflow and creates steam underneath, which ruins browning. Skip it. A drip tray catches rendered fat, which can pool and cause spattering when you open the basket, so if your fryer sits above cabinetry and you\'re worried about grease, a tray on the rack below the fryer catches it—not under the steak itself.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Does a USDA Prime ribeye cook differently than a Choice ribeye?',
        a: 'Prime has more marbling, which means more intramuscular fat to render during cooking. It reaches 135°F slightly faster (9–11 minutes instead of 10–13) because fat conducts heat better than muscle fiber. The time window still overlaps, so you can use the same 10–13 range but check the meat at 9 minutes.',
      },
      {
        q: 'I don\'t have a meat thermometer; how else can I know it\'s 135°F?',
        a: 'Press the meat with your finger and compare the firmness to your thumb pad: touch your thumb to your ring finger and press the pad—that\'s rare (120°F). Touch your middle finger—that\'s medium-rare (135°F). Touch your index finger—that\'s medium (145°F). This feels-based test is less accurate than a probe, but it\'s a useful second opinion if your thermometer is broken.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I cook the ribeye in the air fryer at a lower temperature for longer, like 350°F for 20 minutes, to be extra careful about overcooking?',
        a: 'You can, but you won\'t get the brown, rendered-fat crust that makes air-fryer steaks worth cooking in the fryer. The Maillard reaction requires temperatures above 280°F and is much less efficient below 375°F. A 350°F cook produces a pale, steamed exterior and defeats the purpose. Stick to 400°F and trust the timer.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What\'s the best way to rest the ribeye after cooking?',
        a: 'Let it rest for 3–5 minutes on a warm plate before cutting. The resting period lets the muscle fibers that contracted during heating relax slightly, which helps them hold onto juices when you cut into it. A resting period under 3 minutes is too short to make a difference; over 5 minutes and the surface cools noticeably.',
      },
    ],
    technicalDeepDive: 'A ribeye carries heavy intramuscular marbling that renders as the meat heats, basting the muscle fibers from within and helping conduct heat evenly through a cut this thick. At 400°F, the fryer\'s forced air strips the humid boundary layer off the meat\'s surface far faster than a still oven would, so the exterior climbs past the Maillard threshold within minutes while the core stays far below it. Because meat conducts heat poorly, that gap between crust and center persists through most of the cook, and the interior only reaches 135°F once the crust has already set, keeping the medium-rare band from overshooting.',
