      'Smoking cast iron requires 350°F+ surface temperature minimum at entry. If the iron is merely hot (under 350°F), the roast will cool the pan into the steam range (212°F) before crossing into Maillard browning (280–330°F), wasting the entire sear window. Listen for immediate, crackling sizzle on contact; a muted sizzle signals insufficient preheat.',
      'Tallow (rendered beef fat) conducts heat into the tri-tip\'s thin, unevenly distributed fat cap more efficiently than water or neutral oil. Pre-heat the tallow in the skillet for 30 seconds after reaching smoking temp, then add the roast; immediate sizzle confirms proper heat transfer. Avoid butter or low-smoke-point fats, which break down and transfer heat poorly.',
      'Tri-tip\'s thin wedge geometry produces unequal fat pooling as it renders. Tilt the skillet toward the thicker end of the triangle to pool rendered fat in that direction; it provides conductive heat boost to the slower-cooking apex. The thin side\'s fat will render and drain toward the thicker side naturally.',
