      'Coat the hot pan surface with a combination of butter and a neutral oil (blend roughly 2 parts butter to 1 part avocado or refined vegetable oil) 10-15 seconds before placing the fillet. The oil raises the smoke point of the butter, protecting it from browning too quickly, while butter still provides browning and flavor. Use barely a teaspoon of this mixture per fillet—tilapia\'s delicate surface absorbs cooking fat quickly and too much oil pooling will cause sticking.',
      'Heat the oil-butter mixture only until it shimmers (you\'ll see thin waves across the surface), not until it smokes. Tilapia\'s mild flavor profile is easily overwhelmed by burnt butter or charred oil, and the fish doesn\'t benefit from aggressive heat the way salmon does. The shimmer indicates 325-350°F in the fat itself, still hot enough to sear without damaging the delicate protein.',
