    technicalDeepDive: 'Bone collagen is more densely cross-linked than the collagen in muscle connective tissue, so extracting it into gelatin takes many more hours than even a long meat braise, which is why this broth runs for half a day or longer at a temperature held just under boiling. Roasting the bones first triggers Maillard reactions across the marrow and connective surfaces, building flavor compounds that simmering in water alone would never generate. The cider vinegar\'s mild acidity lowers the pH of the cooking liquid enough to slowly demineralize the bone surface, exposing more collagen to the hot water and speeding its conversion into dissolved gelatin. That extracted gelatin is a tangle of unwound collagen strands that partially reform their coiled structure as the broth cools, which is the physical basis for a rich broth setting up firm in the refrigerator.',
