    technicalDeepDive: 'Roasting at 400°F pushes the skin surface through the Maillard range quickly enough to build deep browning within about an hour, well before the meat underneath is at any real risk of drying out, since the skin sits directly exposed to hot air while the meat beneath heats more slowly by conduction through fat and connective tissue. The bird is roasted to two different endpoints because the two muscles are structurally different: thigh tissue is worked constantly as the bird walks and stands, so it carries enough connective tissue that it actually benefits from the extra heat needed to reach 175°F, while the comparatively idle breast muscle has little of that tissue to convert and turns dry if it climbs much past its 165°F mark. Perching the trussed bird on root vegetables also means those vegetables absorb rendered fat and juices as they roast, shielding the pot\'s base from drippings that would otherwise scorch against hot metal.',
