    technicalDeepDive: 'French green lentils, often called Puy-style, hold a firmer bite than common brown lentils because their outer coat is denser and higher in structural pectin, resisting breakdown even once the starch inside each lentil has fully gelatinized and turned tender. That coat is why this variety tolerates a full simmer without slumping into the pot the way a softer-skinned lentil eventually would, since the starch and the skin are softening on two different timelines rather than one. A gentle, steady simmer rather than a hard boil matters here too: aggressive bubbling knocks lentils against each other and against the pot walls, and that physical abrasion can split a thinner hull just as easily as heat alone.',
