    technicalDeepDive: 'A Dutch baby\'s batter is essentially a thin custard, and pouring it into a pot already heated to 425°F sets the egg proteins at the surface almost on contact instead of warming gradually. That fast protein coagulation traps water vaporizing out of the batter as steam, and since the batter carries little of the structured gluten network a bread dough would, that trapped steam has nowhere to go but up, forcing the thin edges to climb the pot\'s hot walls. The center never touches metal directly, so it sets more slowly from convected air heat and keeps more of its moisture, which is why it stays custardy while the rim turns crisp.',
