      'When you check temperature, insert the probe from the side of the chop and angle the tip toward the center of the loin muscle rather than letting it graze the bone — a reading taken right against the bone reads cooler than the meat around it, which can trick you into leaving the chop in longer than the muscle itself needs.',
      'Pour in just enough cider to come roughly a third to halfway up the sides of the chops sitting on the onion bed, not enough to fully submerge them — submerged chops braise closer to a poach, pulling more surface flavor into the liquid and finishing stringier than the partial-braise, partial-steam effect this method is built around.',
