    technicalDeepDive: 'Thigh meat is dark because it\'s rich in myoglobin and built for sustained movement, which also means it carries far more intramuscular collagen than chicken breast does. That\'s why the target here sits well above the USDA\'s 165°F poultry minimum: at exactly 165°F the collagen in a thigh has barely begun to soften, so the meat would still taste tough despite being fully safe to eat. Carrying it on to around 175°F over five to six hours gives that connective tissue time to convert to gelatin, the same mechanism that tenderizes a beef chuck roast, just on a smaller, faster timescale with far less collagen mass to hydrolyze. The bone also acts as a slower-conducting core, buffering the surrounding meat while the rest of the thigh comes up to temperature through the braising liquid.',
