    technicalDeepDive: 'A whole bird cooking bone-in creates two different thermal endpoints inside one appliance: the bone itself is a poor conductor and acts as a local heat sink, so the dense thigh muscle wrapped around it needs to climb to 175°F before its connective tissue has enough time-and-temperature exposure to soften, while the leaner breast is done as soon as it clears 165°F and any further hold would push its tighter muscle fibers toward dryness. The chicken sits above the pooling drippings, which lets ambient steam trapped by the sealed lid surround the whole carcass in humid, even heat rather than the direct dry-roasting contact an oven would provide.',
