    technicalDeepDive: 'The stew beneath the dumplings heats them by direct conduction from the simmering liquid, but the exposed tops sit above the liquid line and cook mainly by convection from trapped steam and radiant heat off the lid. Baking powder releases carbon dioxide once it meets moisture and heat, and that gas expands within the gluten network of the drop dough, pushing each dumpling upward as its outer layer\'s starches and proteins set into a stable structure at the same 195°F the stew is held at. Because the bottom half sits in that liquid while the crown cooks in a cooler vapor pocket, each dumpling finishes with a gradient from dense, saturated base to lighter, more cooked crown.',
